<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  sidewalk_robot.urdf.xacro — the robot's body, described so software can read it.

  WHAT A URDF IS
    A simplified CAD model. It does not describe the robot precisely enough to
    manufacture — it describes it precisely enough to REASON about: where each
    part is, how parts connect, and how big they are.

    Two things consume it:
      • robot_state_publisher, which turns it into the live transform tree that
        every other program queries to ask "where is the camera right now?"
      • RViz, which draws it, so you can SEE whether your measurements are right.

    That second use is underrated. Load this in RViz alongside a live point
    cloud: if the ground plane in the cloud does not line up with the wheels in
    the model, a mounting number in robot_frames.yaml is wrong. It is far easier
    to spot a bad transform visually than in a table of numbers.

  LINKS AND JOINTS
    A link is a rigid body. A joint is the relationship between two links.
    Here every joint is "fixed" — nothing on this robot articulates. The wheels
    are drawn as continuous joints so the model is honest about what rotates,
    but the camera mount must be rigid, and any flex in it is a defect.

  WHY THE CAMERA MOUNT MATTERS MECHANICALLY
    SLAM assumes the camera-to-robot transform is CONSTANT. A bracket that
    flexes under vibration creates an unmeasured, time-varying transform. The
    camera and IMU stay internally consistent, so nothing looks obviously
    broken — the pose estimate just quietly degrades.

    A stiff mount is therefore better than a compliant one even on a vibrating
    chassis. A soft bracket does not isolate the camera; it lets the camera
    oscillate independently, which is worse than transmitting the vibration.

  UNITS: metres and radians throughout.
-->
<robot name="sidewalk_robot" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Dimensions. These MUST match config/robot_frames.yaml, which is the
       authoritative source. They are duplicated here because xacro cannot
       read a YAML file directly; if you change one, change both.

       There is a THIRD copy, as a polygon, in
           sidewalk_navigation/config/costmap_common.yaml
       and that one is safety-critical: it is what the planner uses to decide
       whether the robot fits through a gap. It disagreed with
       robot_frames.yaml by 29 cm in length and 17 cm in width before anyone
       noticed, so nav_preflight.py now compares those two at runtime and
       fails on mismatch.

       This URDF copy is NOT covered by that check. It drives visualisation
       and the TF tree rather than collision checking, so a mismatch here is
       misleading rather than dangerous — but it is still a mismatch, and RViz
       showing a robot that is not the size of the robot is exactly the kind
       of thing that makes a real problem hard to see. Keep it in step.

       STATUS: robot_frames.yaml carries `measured: false`. These are
       UNMEASURED PLACEHOLDERS. Measure the robot before it drives.

       THAT FLAG IS SCOPED TO THE CHASSIS, NOT TO THIS WHOLE FILE. It speaks
       for robot_frames.yaml's `base:` block only — the seven dimensions
       immediately below — and the file itself now says so at length. It does
       NOT speak for the front camera pose further down, which WAS measured on
       2026-08-29 and carries its own provenance where it is defined.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:property name="base_length"      value="0.99"/>
  <xacro:property name="base_width"       value="0.67"/>
  <xacro:property name="base_height"      value="0.28"/>
  <xacro:property name="ground_clearance" value="0.13"/>
  <xacro:property name="wheel_radius"     value="0.165"/>
  <xacro:property name="wheel_width"      value="0.10"/>
  <xacro:property name="wheel_separation" value="0.55"/>

  <!-- ZED X physical envelope: 163 x 32 x 37 mm, about 240 g. -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_length" value="0.163"/>
  <xacro:property name="zedx_height" value="0.032"/>
  <xacro:property name="zedx_depth"  value="0.037"/>
  <xacro:property name="zedx_mass"   value="0.240"/>
  <!-- Stereo baseline: the physical distance between the two lenses. This is
       the ruler that gives stereo vision its absolute scale. -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_baseline" value="0.120"/>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FRONT CAMERA POSE — and WHICH PHYSICAL POINT it describes.

       THESE ARE THE MOUNT NUMBERS, NOT THE LENS NUMBERS. Read that before
       editing them. The mount point and the left lens are 60 mm apart in y
       and this project has already been bitten by the difference; that is
       why robot_frames.yaml devotes half a page to it.

       WHICH POINT THIS IS. The zedx_camera macro below hangs ${name}_link
       off base_link using exactly these four values, and ${name}_link is
       the CAMERA HOUSING — it carries the box visual, and both lens frames
       hang off it at plus/minus half the stereo baseline in y. So the point
       positioned here is the MOUNT / housing anchor. It is the same
       physical point as
           zedx_base_frame        in sidewalk_bringup/urdf/husky_a200_real.urdf
           zedx_base_frame        in sidewalk_sim/urdf/husky_real.urdf
           zedx_front_base_link   the ZED driver's own root frame
       and it is NOT the left lens.

       PROVENANCE — measured on this robot, 2026-08-29.
         z = 0.547   tape measured 0.7112 m, floor to the ZED X housing
                     UPPER SURFACE, minus 0.0159 (half the 31.8 mm body
                     height, Stereolabs ZED X datasheet Rev 1.6), minus
                     0.0160 (the camera's internal base-to-centre offset,
                     read from the live transform tree), minus 0.13228
                     (base_link above the floor, from husky_a200_real.urdf's
                     base_footprint_joint).
         y = -0.040  a PHYSICAL re-position of the camera toward
                     swiftnav_link, made the same day. It was +0.070. Do not
                     "restore" the old value — the camera actually moved.
         x = 0.081   from the mount tuned live in simulation.
         pitch = 0.0565 rad = 3.24 degrees NOSE DOWN. Measured twice by two
                     methods that share no failure mode: a RANSAC floor-plane
                     fit (a statistical fit of a flat plane to the depth
                     image's view of the floor, discarding outliers) and the
                     ZED's accelerometer reading of gravity.
         roll and yaw are left at 0, but that is UNMEASURED-AND-SET-TO-ZERO,
                     not measured-as-zero. Nobody has established that this
                     camera is square in those two axes. Do not quote it as
                     if they had.

       THE COMMENT THAT USED TO BE HERE SAID THESE CAME FROM
       robot_frames.yaml. THEY DID NOT, AND THEY STILL DO NOT — and it is
       important that they do not. robot_frames.yaml's camera_front block
       holds the LEFT LENS at (0.071, 0.020, 0.563), which is this mount
       point PLUS the ZED's internal base-to-left-lens offset
       (-0.010, +0.060, +0.016). Different point, on purpose, because the
       ZED SDK reports depth from the left lens. Copying robot_frames.yaml's
       x/y/z into these four properties would put the housing 60 mm to the
       robot's left of where it physically is, and drag both lens frames
       with it.

       SUPERSEDED VALUES — must not reappear here or anywhere else:
           x=0.35  y=0.00  z=0.62  pitch=0.087   what was in this file until
                                                 2026-08-29, never measured
           x=0.25  z=0.60                        sidewalk_multicam placeholders
           z=0.558 / z=0.578                     intermediate working values
           y=+0.070                              superseded; camera moved

       ── THE POSE NOW LIVES IN FOUR PLACES AND THEY MOVE TOGETHER ────────
       Nothing compares them at runtime. Change one and not the others and
       the robot model and the camera driver quietly describe different
       robots; no node complains, the map just comes out wrong.

         sidewalk_perception/launch/zedx_front.launch
             cam_pos_x / cam_pos_y / cam_pos_z / cam_pitch
             THE ONE WITH TEETH. It positions the ZED driver's entire frame
             tree, and RTAB-Map uses the driver's frames, not this file's.
             These were 0.0 until 2026-08-29, which put every observation in
             runs 1 through 6 about 0.54 m too low.
         sidewalk_bringup/urdf/husky_a200_real.urdf   zedx_base_joint
             real robot, RViz.
         sidewalk_sim/urdf/husky_real.urdf            zedx_base_joint
             simulation. Same physical mount, described twice.
         sidewalk_bringup/config/robot_frames.yaml    camera_front
             the LEFT LENS — this plus (-0.010, +0.060, +0.016). Deliberately
             different numbers; see above before "fixing" them to match.

       This file is the fifth copy, and that is the defect rather than the
       feature. See the FRAME NAME COLLISION warning further down, just above
       where the front camera is instantiated.

       VERIFY after changing any of them — on the robot, camera running:
           rosrun tf tf_echo zedx_base_frame zedx_front_base_link
       must read about [0, 0, 0] in BOTH translation and rotation. That is
       the robot model's mount point and the driver's root landing on top of
       each other, which is the only thing that proves the copies agree.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_front_x"     value="0.081"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_front_y"     value="-0.040"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_front_z"     value="0.547"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_front_pitch" value="0.0565"/>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       REAR CAMERA POSE — PLACEHOLDERS, NOT GEOMETRY. Phase 7.

       These have their own properties rather than reusing cam_front_z and
       cam_front_pitch, which is what this file did before 2026-08-29. That
       reuse was harmless while the front numbers were themselves guesses;
       it stopped being harmless the moment the front numbers became
       MEASURED, because the rear camera would then silently inherit a
       height and a tilt measured on a bracket at the other end of the robot
       — measured-looking numbers describing hardware that does not exist.

       The values below deliberately mirror robot_frames.yaml's camera_rear
       block, placeholders and all, so the two files say the same untrue
       thing rather than two different untrue things. They are the same
       superseded x=-0.35 / z=0.62 / pitch=0.087 that sat on the front
       camera until it was measured.

       MEASURE THEM ON THE REAR BRACKET when the hardware is fitted, with
       the two methods recorded in robot_frames.yaml camera_front. Do NOT
       copy the front camera's numbers across and negate x: that is a
       different bracket, in a different place, at a different height.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_rear_x"     value="-0.35"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_rear_y"     value="0.00"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_rear_z"     value="0.62"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_rear_pitch" value="0.087"/>

  <!-- Set to true when the Phase 7 rear camera is physically installed.

       THIS CANNOT BE SET FROM THE COMMAND LINE, AND bringup.launch BELIEVES IT
       CAN. Edit the value here; passing it in does nothing.

       sidewalk_bringup/launch/bringup.launch builds /robot_description with
           $(find xacro)/xacro '...sidewalk_robot.urdf.xacro'
                    rear_camera_installed:=$(arg rear_camera)
       and that assignment is SILENTLY DISCARDED. A xacro command-line
       `name:=value` only ever binds a <xacro:arg>, read back as $(arg name).
       This is a <xacro:property>, which is a different namespace; xacro does
       not warn about a command-line assignment that matches nothing, and it
       exits 0.

       [VERIFIED 2026-08-29 on a development computer, in the local sidewalk-sim:noetic
       container, against this exact file:
           xacro sidewalk_robot.urdf.xacro rear_camera_installed:=true \
             | grep -c zedx_rear     ->  0
        with empty stderr and exit status 0. The file contains no <xacro:arg>
        declarations at all.]

       WHAT THAT COSTS TODAY: nothing, because the hardware does not exist.
       WHAT IT COSTS ON THE DAY IT IS FITTED: `bringup.launch rear_camera:=true`
       starts the rear ZED driver (it is a plain roslaunch `if`, so that half
       works) while the robot model quietly stays single-camera. The rear
       driver's frames then hang in the tree with no link to base_link, and the
       failure surfaces as TF lookup errors far from this file.

       DELIBERATELY NOT "FIXED" BY PROMOTING IT TO A <xacro:arg>. Making the
       flag work would let one command-line word inject the cam_rear_*
       PLACEHOLDERS above — x=-0.35, z=0.62, pitch=0.087, which describe no
       bracket that exists — into the live transform tree, where they would be
       indistinguishable from the measured front-camera numbers beside them.
       The present behaviour fails SAFE: the rear camera cannot appear until
       somebody edits this line, and whoever edits this line is looking
       directly at the block above telling them to measure the bracket first.
       Promote it to a <xacro:arg> at the same time as replacing those
       placeholders with measurements, not before. -->
  <xacro:property name="rear_camera_installed" value="false"/>

  <xacro:property name="PI" value="3.14159265359"/>

  <material name="grey">  <color rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1.0"/></material>
  <material name="black"> <color rgba="0.10 0.10 0.10 1.0"/></material>
  <material name="blue">  <color rgba="0.15 0.40 0.80 1.0"/></material>
  <material name="orange"><color rgba="0.90 0.50 0.10 1.0"/></material>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       base_link — the root of everything.

       Positioned at the centre of rotation, projected to the ground: the
       midpoint of the drive axle, at z = 0. The differential-drive odometry
       equations assume exactly this, so moving it silently biases odometry.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <link name="base_link"/>

  <link name="chassis_link">
    <visual>
      <geometry><box size="${base_length} ${base_width} ${base_height}"/></geometry>
      <material name="grey"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <geometry><box size="${base_length} ${base_width} ${base_height}"/></geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <mass value="35.0"/>
      <!-- Approximate box inertia. Adequate for visualisation and for a
           first-pass EKF; replace with measured or CAD-derived values before
           making any dynamic claim. -->
      <inertia ixx="1.5" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="2.8" iyz="0.0" izz="3.2"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>

  <joint name="base_to_chassis" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child  link="chassis_link"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 ${ground_clearance + base_height/2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── wheels ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <xacro:macro name="wheel" params="prefix y_offset x_offset">
    <link name="${prefix}_wheel_link">
      <visual>
        <origin rpy="${PI/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="${wheel_radius}" length="${wheel_width}"/></geometry>
        <material name="black"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin rpy="${PI/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="${wheel_radius}" length="${wheel_width}"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="2.5"/>
        <inertia ixx="0.02" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="0.02" iyz="0" izz="0.03"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${prefix}_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
      <parent link="chassis_link"/>
      <child  link="${prefix}_wheel_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${x_offset} ${y_offset} ${-base_height/2 - ground_clearance + wheel_radius}"/>
      <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <xacro:wheel prefix="front_left"  y_offset="${ wheel_separation/2}" x_offset="0.26"/>
  <xacro:wheel prefix="front_right" y_offset="${-wheel_separation/2}" x_offset="0.26"/>
  <xacro:wheel prefix="rear_left"   y_offset="${ wheel_separation/2}" x_offset="-0.26"/>
  <xacro:wheel prefix="rear_right"  y_offset="${-wheel_separation/2}" x_offset="-0.26"/>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZED X camera macro.

       THE OPTICAL FRAME IS THE SUBTLE PART. Read this before changing it.

       ROS bodies use x-forward, y-left, z-up (REP-103).
       Cameras conventionally use z-FORWARD (out of the lens), x-right,
       y-DOWN (REP-104).

       These two conventions disagree, and the rotation between them is the
       single most common source of "my point cloud is rotated 90 degrees and
       lying on its side". The fixed rpy="-pi/2 0 -pi/2" below is exactly that
       conversion. It is not a fudge factor and must not be tuned.

       We publish BOTH frames: a body-convention *_link for mechanical
       reasoning, and a *_optical_frame for image and depth data.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:macro name="zedx_camera" params="name x y z roll pitch yaw colour">

    <!-- Housing, in body convention. -->
    <link name="${name}_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry><box size="${zedx_depth} ${zedx_length} ${zedx_height}"/></geometry>
        <material name="${colour}"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <geometry><box size="${zedx_depth} ${zedx_length} ${zedx_height}"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="${zedx_mass}"/>
        <inertia ixx="0.0003" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="0.0001" iyz="0" izz="0.0003"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="base_to_${name}" type="fixed">
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child  link="${name}_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${x} ${y} ${z}" rpy="${roll} ${pitch} ${yaw}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Left lens. The SDK reports depth relative to THIS point, which is why
         robot_frames.yaml insists you measure to the left lens.

         APPROXIMATE, AND KNOWINGLY SO. This models the housing-to-lens offset
         as pure half-baseline in y. The ZED X's real internal offset from its
         base to the left lens, read from the live transform tree on the
         Jetson, is (-0.010, +0.060, +0.016) — it also steps 10 mm back and
         16 mm up, and this macro captures neither. So with the mount set
         correctly above, the frame produced here sits about 10 mm forward of
         and 16 mm below the real left lens, which robot_frames.yaml records
         at (0.071, 0.020, 0.563) relative to base_link.

         That is fine for making the model look right in RViz and it is NOT a
         number to compute with. The measured lens position is in
         robot_frames.yaml camera_front; the accurate live frame is published
         by the ZED driver itself — which is also what collides with this one.
         See the FRAME NAME COLLISION warning below. -->
    <link name="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_left_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_link"/>
      <child  link="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 ${zedx_baseline/2} 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Body convention -> optical convention. Do not tune this. -->
    <link name="${name}_left_camera_optical_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_left_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
      <child  link="${name}_left_camera_optical_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-PI/2} 0 ${-PI/2}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Right lens, one baseline away. Depth resolution derives directly from
         this separation: Z = f*B/d, so a wider B improves distant depth. -->
    <link name="${name}_right_camera_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_right_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_link"/>
      <child  link="${name}_right_camera_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 ${-zedx_baseline/2} 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${name}_right_camera_optical_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_right_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_right_camera_frame"/>
      <child  link="${name}_right_camera_optical_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-PI/2} 0 ${-PI/2}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- IMU, inside the housing. The SDK knows the precise internal offset;
         this frame exists so the transform tree is complete and so fusion
         nodes have somewhere to attach. -->
    <link name="${name}_imu_link"/>
    <joint name="${name}_imu_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_link"/>
      <child  link="${name}_imu_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WARNING — FIVE OF THE FRAME NAMES BELOW COLLIDE WITH THE ZED DRIVER'S.

       This is a live conflict, not a hypothetical one, and nothing reports it
       at runtime. Correcting the mount numbers above makes the two
       descriptions AGREE TODAY; it does not remove the conflict.

       HOW BOTH END UP RUNNING. bringup.launch loads this file into
       /robot_description and starts robot_state_publisher. With camera:=true
       it ALSO includes sidewalk_perception/launch/zedx_front.launch, whose
       include of the wrapper's zed_camera.launch.xml starts a SECOND
       robot_state_publisher — node name "<camera_name>_state_publisher" —
       for the driver's own description, zed_descr.urdf.xacro. That is gated
       on publish_urdf, which DEFAULTS TO true and which zedx_front.launch
       does not override. Static transforms go to the global /tf_static
       regardless of the launch namespace, so both land in one tree.
       [VERIFIED 2026-08-29 by reading zed_camera.launch.xml,
       zed_macro.urdf.xacro and the wrapper nodelet source on the JETSON.]

       THE FIVE COLLIDING NAMES, and the different parents each side gives them:

         frame name                              here            ZED driver
         ─────────────────────────────────────── ─────────────── ──────────────────────
         zedx_front_left_camera_frame            zedx_front_link zedx_front_camera_center
         zedx_front_left_camera_optical_frame    (left frame)    (left frame)
         zedx_front_right_camera_frame           zedx_front_link zedx_front_camera_center
         zedx_front_right_camera_optical_frame   (right frame)   (right frame)
         zedx_front_imu_link                     zedx_front_link driver node, at runtime
                                                                 (mImuFrameId =
                                                                  camera_name+"_imu_link")

       A frame in a transform tree may have exactly ONE parent. Two authorities
       publishing the same child is not something TF raises an error about — it
       is a race, and which description a given lookup resolves through depends
       on publication order. Every distance computed through those frames is
       then referenced to whichever one won, silently.

       ${name}_link itself does NOT collide: the driver's macro declares no
       bare ${name}_link. It declares ${name}_base_link, ${name}_camera_center,
       the four lens frames above, and optional mag/baro/temp links; the node
       adds ${name}_imu_link.

       THE REAL FIX IS TO DELETE THE CAMERA FROM THIS FILE and let the driver
       own those frames — one physical camera should have one description.
       That is NOT done here because it is a judgement call with a real cost:
       the camera stops appearing in RViz whenever the driver is not running,
       which is exactly when someone is checking mounting geometry by eye, and
       it would need the housing kept as a visual-only link under a
       non-colliding name. It is written up for the user to decide.

       UNTIL THAT DECISION IS MADE, the four numbers above must track the four
       other copies of this pose listed in the mount-pose block near the top of
       this file. They only agree because somebody kept them agreeing.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- Front camera — installed. MOUNT point, not the lens. See above. -->
  <xacro:zedx_camera name="zedx_front"
                     x="${cam_front_x}" y="${cam_front_y}" z="${cam_front_z}"
                     roll="0" pitch="${cam_front_pitch}" yaw="0"
                     colour="blue"/>

  <!-- Rear camera — Phase 7. Facing backwards (yaw = pi).
       Guarded so the model stays truthful about what is physically present.
       Uses the cam_rear_* PLACEHOLDERS, deliberately not the front camera's
       measured values — see the rear-camera block near the top of this file. -->
  <xacro:if value="${rear_camera_installed}">
    <xacro:zedx_camera name="zedx_rear"
                       x="${cam_rear_x}" y="${cam_rear_y}" z="${cam_rear_z}"
                       roll="0" pitch="${cam_rear_pitch}" yaw="${PI}"
                       colour="orange"/>
  </xacro:if>

</robot>
